Nginx Proxy Manager (NPM) on Proxmox

Notes for setting up Nginx Proxy Manager as an LXC container on Proxmox, so my self-hosted apps open by name (like vault.example.com) instead of IP address and port.

Based on this video: Nginx Proxy Manager Complete Guide - Proxmox Homelab Setup 2026 by Barmine Tech.

Placeholders used in this file: example.com is my domain, 192.168.1.80 is the NPM container.

What a reverse proxy does

Traffic to a name hits the proxy first. The proxy can allow it, drop it, filter it or add security, then forward it to the right app.

vault.example.com  ->  NPM (192.168.1.80)  ->  192.168.1.82:8000

Benefits:

No need to remember IP addresses and ports.
One wildcard certificate covers every service, which removes the browser security warning.
Easy for other people in the house to use.
1. Install NPM (Proxmox helper script)
Open the Proxmox VE Helper-Scripts website and find the Nginx Proxy Manager script.
Open a shell on the Proxmox host, paste the install command from the site, and run it.
Choose the Default install. It finishes quickly.
Copy the address it prints and open it in a browser, on port 81:
http://192.168.1.80:81

Review a helper script before running it, because it runs as root on the Proxmox host.

2. First login
On first start, NPM asks for a name, an email address and a password.
Sign in. Dark mode is available in the user settings.
Create a second administrator account with a strong password (see Users below).
3. Cloudflare DNS records

In Cloudflare, open the domain, then DNS > Records. Remove anything that could conflict, then add two records:

Type	Name	Content	Proxy status
A	example.com	IPv4 address of the NPM host (192.168.1.80)	DNS only
CNAME	*	example.com	DNS only
Enter only the IP address, with no http:// and no trailing slash.
Turn the proxy off (grey cloud). Cloudflare cannot reach a private address.
Leave TTL on Auto.
DNS can take 5 minutes to half an hour, or longer, to propagate. If a name does not resolve right away, wait and retry.

Check with:

ping nginx.example.com
nslookup nginx.example.com 1.1.1.1
4. Wildcard SSL certificate (Let's Encrypt, DNS challenge)

One certificate for the root domain and the wildcard covers every service.

In Cloudflare, create an API token (profile settings > API Tokens) with Zone > DNS > Edit permission for the domain.
In NPM go to Certificates > Add Certificate > Let's Encrypt via DNS.
Add the domain names:
example.com (click to add it)
*.example.com (click to add it)
Key type: RSA 2048 is widely compatible and fine for internal services. ECDSA is faster and more secure, but not supported everywhere.
DNS provider: Cloudflare. Paste the API token.
Propagation seconds: 60, so the DNS check does not fail.
Save. It takes about a minute.

The certificate list then shows the expiry date and lets you renew or download it from NPM.

Never put the API token in a GitHub repository.

5. Add a proxy host
Go to Hosts > Proxy Hosts > Add Proxy Host.
Domain Names: nginx.example.com. Click to add the name, or it will not be saved.
Scheme: http or https, depending on the app.
Forward Hostname / IP: the app's address, for example 192.168.1.80.
Forward Port: the app's port, for example 81 for the NPM admin page.
Optional: Websockets Support (needed by some apps), Block Common Exploits, Cache Assets.
Custom locations: leave blank.
SSL tab: select the wildcard certificate, then save.

Notes:

"Publicly Accessible" in the Access List field does not mean the app is open to the internet. It only means NPM adds no restriction of its own.
If an app does not load, edit the host and flip the scheme between http and https.
A name that does not resolve yet usually means DNS is still propagating, or the name is misspelled.
6. Example hosts
nginx.example.com     http://192.168.1.80:81
truenas.example.com   https://192.168.1.88:443
vault.example.com     https://192.168.1.82:8000
proxmox.example.com   https://192.168.1.50:8006
pihole.example.com    http://192.168.1.60:80

Turn on Websockets Support for TrueNAS, Proxmox and Pi-hole. Pi-hole only serves /admin, so open https://pihole.example.com/admin/.

7. Other NPM features
Feature	What it does
Redirection Hosts	Sends visitors from an old URL to a new one automatically.
Streams	Forwards a TCP or UDP port to another machine and port (game servers, FTP and similar).
404 Hosts	Shows a 404 page for a name you do not want others to reach, with an optional custom message.
Access Lists	Restricts who can reach a host by username and password, IP address or subnet.
Users	Multiple accounts. Keep two administrators with strong passwords, in case a password is lost or the database is corrupted.
Audit Log	Shows who changed what, including the generated config. Useful when several people manage the proxy.
Settings	Edit the default site (the "Congratulations" page) shown for unknown names.
Access list example
Go to Access Lists > Add Access List.
Add a username and password and/or allow a specific IP or subnet.
Open a proxy host, set the Access List field to it, and save.
The site now asks for a login before it loads.
8. Security notes
Keep the NPM admin page (port 81) off the internet.
If you ever expose NPM to the internet, follow proper security practices first. The video does not cover this.
Use strong, unique passwords and turn on 2FA for Cloudflare and every service behind the proxy.
Reserve the NPM container's address and the apps' addresses on the router so the forward targets do not change.
Troubleshooting
Symptom	Cause and fix
Name does not resolve	DNS is still propagating, or the name is misspelled. Wait and retry.
Cloudflare returns its own IPs (104.x, 172.67.x)	The record is proxied. Set it to DNS only.
502 Bad Gateway	Wrong scheme, IP or port. Flip http and https, and test the backend directly.
504 or timeout	NPM cannot reach the backend, or the container is offline.
Certificate warning	The wildcard certificate is not selected on the host's SSL tab, or it needs renewing.
Console or shell will not open	Websockets Support is off on that host.
Name works for me but not for others	The other device is not using the DNS that returns the NPM address.
Optional: private access over Tailscale (my own addition, not in the video)

My router returns no address for names that point to a private (192.168.x) address. To keep the sites private and still reachable from anywhere, I gave the NPM container its own Tailscale address and pointed the DNS record at it.

On the Proxmox host, allow the TUN device for the container (ID 101 here):

nano /etc/pve/lxc/101.conf

Add at the bottom:

lxc.cgroup2.devices.allow: c 10:200 rwm
lxc.mount.entry: /dev/net/tun dev/net/tun none bind,create=file

Restart the container and install Tailscale inside it:

pct reboot 101
pct enter 101
curl -fsSL https://tailscale.com/install.sh | sh
tailscale up
tailscale ip -4
exit

Then:

Set the Cloudflare A record to the 100.x.x.x address from tailscale ip -4 (DNS only). The wildcard CNAME stays the same.
In the Tailscale admin console, open the container's menu and choose Disable key expiry.
Test with Tailscale on: https://vault.example.com. With Tailscale off, it should fail, which confirms it is private.

From the host, use pct exec 101 -- tailscale ip -4 (the -- stops pct reading -4 as its own option).

Never commit

Cloudflare API tokens, Tailscale auth keys, tunnel tokens, passwords, .env files, or exported NPM configs.
